          {
            "id": 955,
            "sort": 2,
            "name": "Overview",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"LhvxdlwoRowrFWxCj4llb7Acgje\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-SwKqdUlAjoGrbRxTQHHl8hTXg2e\">Overall, with the exception of country B, the percentage of young people pursuing higher education increased in Countries A, C, and D throughout the period shown. Additionally, Country D and Country A demonstrated the most significant growth, with both reaching the highest percentages by 2010.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 16228,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 956,
            "sort": 3,
            "name": "Body 1",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"LhvxdlwoRowrFWxCj4llb7Acgje\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-Ok04d7hgGo13rUxolv0l6RLdgUg\">Regarding the countries with marked upward trends, in 2000, Country A had the lowest percentage of young people attending university at 35%, whereas Country D recorded a slightly higher figure of 45%. Subsequently, the proportion in Country A subsequently rose considerably to 48% in 2005 and continued this upward trajectory to reach 57% by the end of the period. Similarly, Country D showed steady growth from 45% to 50% over the first five years, before climbing more dramatically to 60% in 2010, thereby registering the highest overall percentage among all four countries.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 16228,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 957,
            "sort": 4,
            "name": "Body 2",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"LhvxdlwoRowrFWxCj4llb7Acgje\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-NVwLdsbqbo7vDUxoW05lxZ6egnh\">As for the remaining nations, Country B and Country C began with fairly similar proportions in 2000, with around 41% of their youth population in higher education on average. Thereafter, country B's percentage then experienced minimal fluctuation, dropping marginally to 39% in 2005 before returning to its original level of 40% by the end of the decade. Meanwhile, Country C saw a modest increase to 47% in 2005, after which its figure plateaued, remaining unchanged at 47% in 2010.</div>\n</div>",
